c87dd0bf1afc581ce84c60d.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=5$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $Q,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-321918/field-1" "q-321918/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321918"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120730/selection/4619abde1e5c3353c992c5a5960a8ca7bbc7aa763cb9c902ac3449ba76e0591c" :answer/id #uuid "0ef23603-c69c-5c86-98b6-b3b474af5491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{8},\\sqrt{8})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120730/selection/b4f2dfc015134669797f2bc4a16d236bf034b61767992098065383cee8eca095" :answer/id #uuid "62f948f7-e73c-544d-b013-254d21436b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120730/selection/b3945c1a19f8eef920bddb368ec675e7e4ef51fce8d90fd87ec4e47a1f7c50e2" :answer/id #uuid "ea6afa2b-1c19-5610-bf4f-dd49940d15ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120730/selection/e8b6e48d2640b190477b067d73ed38c8ae23d6a2118701d65bc1f8aac439d661" :answer/id #uuid "17c23022-ed3c-5fa5-8696-39c