  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120730/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120730"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5389"] :question/problem "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5390"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5391"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5392"] :question/problem "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
